---
import { PLAN_TABLE } from '@studpilot/shared';
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE } from '../../lib/studio-plugin';

// The free allowance is read from the shared product contract so this guide cannot outlive a quota change independently.
/*
 * REWRITTEN FOR THE BETA (M2 site fix cycle 1, plan step 2.6). What it says, and where each claim was checked:
 *   - sign-in: email and a password, or Sign in with Roblox (live since 2026-10-05, owner decision D-10; the worker serves /auth/roblox and the
 *     app's sign-in page links /auth/roblox/start). Google and Discord are not on the sign-in page (nothing in apps/web calls an OAuth sign-in yet).
 *   - the pairing dialog is the app's "Studio connection" window (apps/web/src/components/pairing-dialog.tsx): "Your pairing code", a countdown, and
 *     the instruction this page repeats ("click StudPilot in the Plugins tab, enter this code and press Connect to StudPilot").
 *   - a code lasts 10 minutes and works once (apps/worker/src/do/pairing.ts, TTL_MS); the pairing itself lasts until Studio closes (the plugin's own
 *     first line: "A pairing lasts until Studio closes; after a restart, enter a new code from your StudPilot project"), and the session token
 *     expires after 30 days (do/session.ts, PLUGIN_TOKEN_TTL_MS). The old Connect page said the connection "survives restarts"; the plugin says it
 *     does not, so this page does not.
 *   - edits start off and are turned on in two steps in the panel (apps/studpilot-plugin/src/init.server.luau); tests/lib/plugin-promises.mjs holds it.
 *   - THE METADATA IS ASKED OF THE FLAG TOO, not just the body: a <meta name="description"> is what a search result and a pasted link show, and it
 *     must never promise an install path the body denies (tests/plugin-availability-metadata.test.mjs).
 */
const free = PLAN_TABLE.free;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Getting started | StudPilot"
  description={STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE
    ? 'From a new account to your first request: sign in, make a project, pair the Studio plugin and ask for a piece.'
    : 'From a new account to your first request: sign in, make a project and ask in the chat. Public Studio installation is unavailable right now, so the Studio steps apply only to someone who already has the plugin.'}
  heading="Getting started"
  order={1}
  summary="From a new account to your first request, and what you cannot do yet."
>
  <p>
    StudPilot is in beta. This page takes you from a new account to your first request, and says plainly what does not work yet.
  </p>

  {!STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE && <div class="callout"><p><strong>Public Studio installation is unavailable right now.</strong> Steps 1 to 3 work today. Steps 4 and 5 need the Studio plugin, which new customers cannot get yet. <a href="/docs/plugin">Where the plugin stands</a>.</p></div>}

  <h2>1. Make an account</h2>
  <p>
    Go to <a href="/app">the app</a> and sign up with an email address and a password, or use Sign in with Roblox on the sign-in page. There is no
    card to enter. You start with your full daily allowance of <strong>{free.creditsPerDay} Credits</strong> immediately. Google and Discord
    sign-in are coming. They are not on the sign-in page yet.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Make a project</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>project</strong> is StudPilot's workspace for one Roblox place: its chat, and what you have built together. Press
    <strong>New project</strong> in the app and you land in the project's chat.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Ask for something</h2>
  <p>
    Type a request in the project chat. Short and plain is fine, such as “a chest that opens when you press E”. StudPilot starts work at once, and
    the chat shows a live list of the steps it takes. You can stop a run at any time.
  </p>
  {!STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE && <p>Without the plugin, StudPilot can plan the piece with you in the chat. It cannot change your place in Studio.</p>}

  <h2 id="pair">4. {STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE ? 'Pair the Studio plugin' : 'Pair the plugin, if you already have it'}</h2>
  <p>
    {STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE
      ? <>StudPilot changes your place through the <strong>StudPilot Studio</strong> plugin. It is free on the Roblox Creator Store: follow <a href="/docs/plugin">The Studio plugin</a> to get it, then come back here.</>
      : <>StudPilot changes your place through the <strong>StudPilot Studio</strong> plugin, and there is no working public Creator Store install right now. If you do not already have the plugin, stop here and use the chat. <a href="/docs/plugin">The Studio plugin</a> says where it stands. If you do have it:</>}
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the place you want StudPilot to work on in Roblox Studio, and click <strong>StudPilot</strong> in the Plugins tab. The panel reads <strong>Not connected</strong>.</li>
    <li>In the web app, open your project and open the <strong>Studio connection</strong> window. It shows a six-character code with a countdown.</li>
    <li>Type the code into the panel in Studio and press <strong>Connect to StudPilot</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The panel then reads <strong>Connected</strong> and the name of your project. A code works once and expires after 10 minutes. If yours does, ask
    for a new one. Treat the code as a temporary credential: type it only into your own StudPilot plugin and do not share it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A pairing lasts until Studio closes. After a restart, enter a new code. One Studio window pairs with one project, so a second window needs its
    own code.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Allow edits, then check the result</h2>
  <p>
    Edits stay off until you allow them for that connection. The panel starts at <strong>Access: inspect only</strong>. Press
    <strong>Enable edits…</strong>, then <strong>Allow edits for this connection</strong>. Press <strong>Turn edits off</strong> at any time, or
    <strong>Disconnect</strong> to end the connection.
  </p>
  <p>
    A chat reply is not proof that your place changed. Look at what changed in Studio, and test it yourself.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Taking a change back:</strong> every change StudPilot makes is its own undo step in Studio (<kbd>Ctrl+Z</kbd> or <kbd>⌘Z</kbd>), so a
      run of several steps needs one press per step. Before a run starts changing your place, StudPilot takes a checkpoint of it when it can, and restoring
      that checkpoint from the project takes the whole run back at once.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/credits-and-limits">Credits &amp; limits</a>: how far {free.creditsPerDay} Credits a day goes.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>: when the panel says something you did not expect.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/faq">FAQ</a>: quick answers.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
